Given a vector v and two indices i and j, the sum from i to j is performed by v[i] + v[i+1] + ... + v[j-1] + v[j]. It can also be performed by precalculating the cumulative sum of v (call it cv), and then the sum from i to j is cv[j] - cv[i]. So changing the transformed parameters block of the change point model from:

transformed parameters {
    vector[T] lp;
    lp <- rep_vector(log_unif, T);
    for (s in 1:T) {
        for (t in 1:T) {
            lp[s] <- lp[s] + poisson_log(D[t], if_else(t < s, e, l));
        }
    }
}

to

transformed parameters {
    vector[T+1] lp_e;
    vector[T+1] lp_l;
    vector[T] lp;

    lp_e[1] <- 0;
    lp_l[1] <- 0;
    for (t in 1:T) {
        lp_e[t + 1] <- lp_e[t] + poisson_log(D[t], e);
        lp_l[t + 1] <- lp_l[t] + poisson_log(D[t], l);
    }

    lp <- rep_vector(log_unif + lp_l[T + 1], T) + head(lp_e, T) - head(lp_l, T);
}

results in a dramatic speed up. On my machine 10k samples of the original took 8m53s and the new one took 33s.

Cool!  Or should I say D'Oh!?  I spent 20+ years before
Stan working on dynamic programming algorithms for natural
language processing and speech recognition!

You can avoid saving lp_e and lp_l by making them local
variables:

  transformed parameters {
    vector[T] lp;

    {

      vector[T+1] lp_e;
      vector[T+1] lp_l;

      lp_e[1] <- 0;
      lp_l[1] <- 0;
      for (t in 1:T) {
        lp_e[t + 1] <- lp_e[t] + poisson_log(D[t], e);
        lp_l[t + 1] <- lp_l[t] + poisson_log(D[t], l);
      }
      lp <- rep_vector(log_unif + lp_l[T + 1], T) + head(lp_e, T) - head(lp_l, T);
    }
  }

At first it looked wrong because both probs were collected
the same way, then I noticed lp_l[T+1] in the rep_vector
and the the subtraction, which is a slick way to calculate the
same value.  (A few operations could still be saved at the edges
like the assignments to 0 and the corresponding additions, but
not worth it for the extra complication --- the way you wrote it
is very clean once you see the subtraction.)

I'll put the new version in the manual with a bit of description
and attribute the improved version to you:

https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/issues/1709#issuecomment-208706085

Thanks!

