It is ordered in some sense, since this is a categorisation based on being negative, zero, or positive. But here we think the negative + positive guys have more in common with each other - and probably switch into each others' buckets more often - than with the zero guys, so I don't think we can really use the structure of ordered logits. Although I may be mistaken on this point, and would be happy to hear a counterargument!

(Also, am I right in thinking that a continuous normal regression would be the "linear probability model" (LPM) which could spit out answers that don't satisfy the axioms of probability? Just want to check my understanding.)

I'd be more inclined to switch to hierarchical dirichlet models before an LPM unless anyone sees a big problem with that...

