messages.body_plain:
As Aleksandar suspected, the current model doesn't correspond to MV Poisson distribution. In the current form it's just the product of marginal distributions, ie. defining joint density of form P(X1,X2,X3) = P(X1)P(X2)P(X3) and therefore missing the dependency structure.

The correct joint density (for M=3) is given in page 36. Basically what you have to do is:

take a sum over product of p(y1)p(y2)p(y3)p(y12)p(y13)p(y23), where each term is Poisson density, over all possible combinations of (y1,y2,y3,y12,y13,y23) such that:
x1 == y1+y12+y13
x2 == y2+y12+y23
x3 == y3+y13+y23

>From there, it's easy to generalize for higher dimensions/different dependency structures, but it's also awfully computationally expensive if M/counts are high. Here is a simple (but not very efficient) example code for the M=3 case:
data generation:

> library(rstan)
>
> N <- 500
>
> l1 <- 1.4; y1 <- rpois(N, l1)
> l2 <- 0.8; y2 <- rpois(N, l2)
> l3 <- 3; y3 <- rpois(N, l3)
>
> l12 <- 1.2; y12 <- rpois(N, l12)
> l13 <- 0.1; y13 <- rpois(N, l13)
> l23 <- 0.5; y23 <- rpois(N, l23)
>
> X <- matrix(0, N, 3)
> X[,1] <- y1+y12+y13
> X[,2] <- y2+y12+y23
> X[,3] <- y3+y13+y23
>
> model <- stan_model("MV_poisson.stan")
> samples <- sampling(model, list(X=X,N=N), chains=1, iter=100)

Stan model code:

> data {
>   int N;
>   int X[N, 3];
> }
>
> parameters {
>   real<lower=0> lambda[6];
> }
>
> model {
>   for(n in 1:N) {
>       real lp[(1+min(X[n,1], X[n,2])) * (1+min(X[n,1], X[n,3])) * (1+min(X[n,2], X[n,3]))];
>       int n_used;
>       n_used <- 0;
>       for(y12 in 0:min(X[n,1], X[n,2]))
>        for(y13 in 0:min(X[n,1], X[n,3]))
>           for(y23 in 0:min(X[n,2], X[n,3]))
>             if((y12+y13) <= X[n,1] && (y12+y23) <= X[n,2] && (y13+y23) <= X[n,3]) {
>               n_used <- n_used + 1;
>               lp[n_used] <- poisson_log(X[n,1]-y12-y13, lambda[1]) +
>                           poisson_log(X[n,2]-y12-y23, lambda[2]) +
>                           poisson_log(X[n,3]-y13-y23, lambda[3]) +
>                           poisson_log(y12, lambda[4]) +
>                           poisson_log(y13, lambda[5]) +
>                           poisson_log(y23, lambda[6]);
>             }
>
>       increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(lp[1:n_used]));
>   }
>
>   lambda ~ gamma(0.1, 0.1);
> }

Hi Stan,

I am modeling a discounting factor (hence lies in the range [0,1]) in a hierarchical fashion. I first used Matt Trick (non-centered reparameterization) for sampling from a normal distribution and then transform them into the [0 1] range with Phi_approx. Code as below.

real disc_mu_pr;
real<lower=0> disc_sd;

vector[nSubjects] disc_raw;
vector<lower=0,upper=1+1e-10>[nSubjects] disc;

for (s in 1:nSubjects) {
  disc[s] <- Phi_approx( disc_mu_pr + disc_sd * disc_raw[s] );
}

disc_mu_pr ~ normal(0,1);
disc_sd    ~ cauchy(0,5);
disc_raw   ~ normal(0,1);

The fitting result shows a bi-modal posterior distribution of the disc parameter for nearly all the participants. I wonder it might because that the group level disc_sd is too large, such that the transformed disc parameter is pushed to both ends (0 and 1). I noticed that the R-hat values are actually not bad (all below 1.02, most are 1.00). I paste some of the summaries and I attach the density and pairs plot in case you may want to have a look.

My question is, is there a way to avoid such a bi-modal posterior distribution? Should I use a more restricted prior for disc_sd (eg. half-normal(0,2)), or should I model the disc parameter as beta distribution and discard the Matt Trick?

I appreciate any suggestions. Thanks!

